-- title:
--   Frobenius twists trivial after a square-zero quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring, $\pi \in \mathcal O$ an element and $r$ a prime. Let $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, and assume: (i) for every monic polynomial $q$ over $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree there is an $x \in \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ with $q(x)$ in the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ — that is, $q$ has a root modulo $\pi$; (ii) $\mathrm{Fr}(x) - x^{r}$ lies in that ideal for all $x$, so $\mathrm{Fr}$ lifts the $r$-th power map modulo $\pi$. Let $B$ and $B_0$ be $\mathcal O$-algebras with the image of $\pi$ in $B_0$ nilpotent, and let $p : B \to B_0$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism whose kernel is square-zero in the sense that $st = 0$ whenever $p(s) = p(t) = 0$. Let $\chi : \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism and $k \in \mathbb Z$; write $\mathrm{frobTwist}$ for the twist $\chi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{k}$, the precomposition of $\chi$ with the $k$-th power of $\mathrm{Fr}$ in the group of $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphisms of $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$. Then $p \circ (\chi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{k}) = p \circ \chi$ implies $\chi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{k} = \chi$. (The hypotheses in fact force either $k = 0$ or $B = 0$.) No surjectivity of $p$ is assumed.
--
--   This is the rigidity statement for Frobenius twists of the coefficient leg $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ in the Čerednik–Drinfeld formalism for the formal upper half plane: a twist that becomes invisible after reduction along a square-zero thickening is already trivial. It is used in the construction of lifts of rigidified pairs for fake elliptic curves, where a parity or rigidification condition on the leg has to be transported along infinitesimal thickenings.
